@@s001 Greedy: the method
@@s002 
@@s003 Greedy: the method
@@s004 A greedy algorithm builds the solution a piece at a time: at each step it takes the best element according to a fixed rule, if it is admissible, and never goes back. It is simple to write. The difficulty is proving that the chosen rule leads to the optimum.
@@s005 The scheme of every greedy
@@s006 Every greedy repeats two decisions, at each step:
@@s007 <b>which is the best element now?</b> (the rule: the shortest, the smallest, the first that finishes…);
@@s008 <b>is it admissible?</b> (it respects the constraints: it does not overlap, it does not create a cycle, it fits in the budget).
@@s009 If it is admissible you take it; otherwise you discard it. You never go back on the choices made.
@@s010 The point that decides the exam is not writing the loop, but <b>proving</b> that the rule leads to the optimum. A wrong greedy looks identical to a right one: that is why one always asks for a counterexample or a proof.
@@s011 Why it works: the exchange argument
@@s012 The proof method that is almost always needed at the exam is the <b>exchange</b> (exchange argument):
@@s013 Start from any optimal solution <b>O</b>.
@@s014 If O differs from the greedy, find the first choice where they differ.
@@s015 Swap the element of O with the one of the greedy. Show that the solution remains valid and does not get worse.
@@s016 Repeat: you reach a solution equal to the greedy, which is therefore optimal.
@@s017 The swap must be free
@@s018 The swap works only if the greedy element does not cost more than the one it replaces. If the objects have different values, the swap can worsen the result: then the problem is not greedy, it is a DP (knapsack).
@@s019 Swapping a choice of the optimum with the one of the greedy
@@s020 The other two ways of proving it
@@s021 <b>Greedy stays ahead</b>: after each step, the greedy solution is at least as good as that of anyone else. It is the proof of interval scheduling.
@@s022 <b>Structural bound</b>: you find a bound that every solution must respect, and show that the greedy reaches it. Example: the number of rooms needed is at least the maximum depth of the intervals, and the greedy uses exactly that number.
@@s023 How to recognise a greedy problem
@@s024 In the exams the signal is almost always this: <b>maximise the number of objects under a single budget</b>, or <b>choose the k best</b>, or <b>scheduling on a line</b>. If the objects have different values and a budget on the sum of the values, it is knapsack (DP).
@@s025 Four recurring templates: <i>count under budget</i> (sort by increasing cost, take while it fits); <i>top-k</i> (sort by decreasing value, take the first k); <i>threshold</i> (the minimum number to exceed half of the total: sort decreasing); <i>scheduling</i> (intervals by end, rooms by start).
@@s026 At the exam
@@s027 At the exam
@@s028 Write in this order: (a) the problem; (b) the rule in precise words, <i>"in increasing order of key"</i>, then the pseudocode with the admissibility test; (c) the time, in the parameters of the text: sorting <code>O(n log n)</code> plus scan <code>O(n)</code>. Correctness phrase: <i>"exchange argument: replacing a chosen item by a cheaper unchosen one never hurts."</i>
@@s029 Typical mistake: saying "greedy" without proving it. If you cannot find the exchange argument, it is probably DP.
